    {
      "id": "LA-C286-navis",
      "sequence": 15600,
      "title": "nāvis, nāvis — a ship",
      "headword": "nāvis, nāvis",
      "romanization": "nāvis, nāvis",
      "gloss": "a ship",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:b95a7c9089dd5dd8",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"I am making a journey\". (Iter faciō.) Then \"to sail\", and \"a sailor\". (Nāvigāre. Nauta.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A long Roman journey often went by sea. The word for what the sailor sails has been hiding inside nāvigāre all along."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: nāvis, nāvis",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nāvis, nāvis — \"a ship\". It is feminine, and its accusative is nāvem."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In nāvem ascendō. — I go on board the ship. Nāvis solvit. — The ship sets sail."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You own ascendō, \"to climb, to go up\", and Romans went up into a ship. And you own solvō, \"to untie\": a ship that solvit has cast off its ropes from the shore."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart: nāvis",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nāvis is inherited, usually traced to Proto-Indo-European néh₂us, \"boat\" (EtymDB 2.1). Greek naus, \"ship\", is a cousin (Lewis & Short set them side by side). From the Latin come English naval, navy and the nave of a church, and nāvigō is nāvis with agō, \"to drive\" (Lewis & Short): to drive a ship."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "nāvis, nāvis",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *nāvis, nāvis*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "In nāvem ascendō.",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *In nāvem ascendō.*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Nāvis solvit.",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Nāvis solvit.*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Herī nāvis solvit, et diū nāvigāvimus.",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Herī nāvis solvit, et diū nāvigāvimus.*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the journey and the ship — iter, then nāvis",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the journey and the ship — *iter*, then *nāvis*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does nāvis, nāvis mean? (A ship.) Say \"I go on board\". (In nāvem ascendō.) Say \"the ship sets sail\". (Nāvis solvit.) Which Greek word is its cousin? (Naus.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "LA-C286-naulum",
      "sequence": 15610,
      "title": "naulum, naulī — the fare",
      "headword": "naulum, naulī",
      "romanization": "naulum, naulī",
      "gloss": "a fare, passage money for a ship",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:a1acccd3c53c215e",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"I go on board the ship\". (In nāvem ascendō.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Romans had no tickets. A passenger found a ship going his way and paid its master a fare."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: naulum, naulī",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "naulum, naulī — \"a fare, passage money\". It is neuter, like pretium, \"a price\", which is the word for what it costs."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Naulum solvō. — I pay the fare. Decem dēnāriōs solvī. — I paid ten denarii."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Solvō does both jobs of this chapter: it unties a ship from the shore, and it pays what you owe."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The man you pay is the magister nāvis, \"the master of the ship\": the same magister as a teacher, the one in charge."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart: naulum",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "naulum was borrowed from Greek naulon, \"passage money\" (Lewis & Short), a word usually connected with naus, \"ship\", the Greek cousin of nāvis. The satirist Juvenal uses it, and so do the Roman law books, for the money owed to a ship; the law books also name the magister nāvis, the ship's master (Lewis & Short)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "naulum, naulī",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *naulum, naulī*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Naulum solvō.",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Naulum solvō.*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Prīmum naulum solvī; deinde in nāvem ascendī.",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Prīmum naulum solvī; deinde in nāvem ascendī.*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Magister nāvis decem dēnāriōs accēpit. — The ship's master took ten denarii",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Magister nāvis decem dēnāriōs accēpit.* — The ship's master took ten denarii]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "on land — raeda, a travelling carriage — then by sea: nāvis and naulum",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: on land — *raeda*, a travelling carriage — then by sea: *nāvis* and *naulum*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does naulum, naulī mean? (A fare, for a ship.) Say \"I pay the fare\". (Naulum solvō.) Who takes it? (The magister nāvis.) Which language lent Latin the word? (Greek.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "LA-C286-amitto",
      "sequence": 15620,
      "title": "āmittō — something went wrong",
      "headword": "āmittō, āmittere",
      "romanization": "āmittō, āmittere",
      "gloss": "to lose, to let slip",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:c3fe6b9e1f935301",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ask \"what happened?\" (Quid accidit?) Then say \"I paid the fare\". (Naulum solvī.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "On a journey, the answer to quid accidit is often bad news."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: āmittō, āmittere",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "āmittō, āmittere — \"to lose, to let slip\". Its perfect is āmīsī, \"I have lost\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Sarcinam āmīsī! — I have lost my pack! Occāsiōnem āmīsī. — I let the chance slip."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You own perdō, \"to lose\", too, and the two are not the same. Perdō is losing by wasting or wrecking: money thrown away, a thing ruined. Āmittō is losing by letting go: it slipped away from you."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: reporting the problem",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A problem on the road is an event, so it comes in the perfect:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "— Quid accidit? — What happened? — Nāvis iam discessit! — The ship has already left!"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Discessit is the perfect of discēdō, \"to go away\", and iam, \"already\", makes it the missed boat. Answer quid accidit with what happened, then say what it means for you: Sarcinam āmīsī; nummōs nōn habeō. I have lost my pack; I have no money."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart: āmittō",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "āmittō is built from ab, \"away\", and mittō, \"to send\" (EtymDB 2.1; Lewis & Short): to send away from yourself. In early Latin it meant \"to let someone go\", and from there \"to let a thing slip\", and so \"to lose\". Its perfect āmīsī keeps the perfect of mittō, mīsī."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "āmittō, āmittere — then āmīsī",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *āmittō, āmittere* — then *āmīsī*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Sarcinam āmīsī!",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Sarcinam āmīsī!*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Quid accidit? — Nāvis iam discessit!",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Quid accidit? — Nāvis iam discessit!*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Naulum solvī, sed nāvis iam discessit.",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Naulum solvī, sed nāvis iam discessit.*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "a problem of your own on a journey, and what it means for you",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: a problem of your own on a journey, and what it means for you]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does āmittō, āmittere mean? (To lose, to let slip.) Say \"I have lost my pack\". (Sarcinam āmīsī.) Say \"the ship has already left\". (Nāvis iam discessit.) Which verb loses by wasting? (Perdō.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
